      "id": 1,
      "text": "Gillette sprang to life in 1891 when the Chicago, Burlington & Quincy Railroad extended its tracks to the area, naming the new town after surveyor Edward Gillette.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        3,
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gillette%2C_Wyoming",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Before its 1891 founding, the settlement was known as Donkey Town, a name taken from nearby Donkey Creek.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gillette%2C_Wyoming",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Gillette brands itself the Energy Capital of the Nation, a nod to Wyoming’s contribution of almost 35 percent of the country’s coal.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gillette%2C_Wyoming",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The city hosts a public library that serves as a branch of the Campbell County Public Library System, keeping residents connected to books and community events.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gillette%2C_Wyoming",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Travel Stories lets visitors take virtual tours of downtown Gillette, uncovering the town’s history from the comfort of their car.",
